CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

(1) The implementation of protection of women and children is carried out based on the principles of:

a. respect for the rights of women and children;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination in the empowerment of women and children;
d. the best interests of women and children; and
e. restoration of social and economic rights for women and children who experience acts of violence or neglect.
(2) The objectives of implementing protection of women and children include:
a. preventing acts of violence or neglect against women and children including trafficking in persons;
b. eliminating all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children;
c. protecting and providing security for women and children; d. providing services to women and children victims of acts of violence, reporters, and witnesses; facilitating and mediating e. domestic disputes to realize harmonious and prosperous household integrity; and seeking compensation/compensation f. for losses suffered by victims from perpetrators of violence.

Article 6

The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 5 letter a, include: a. implementing the obligations for protection of women and children from acts of violence established by the government; establishing policies, programs and activities b. for protection of women and children from acts of violence; conducting cooperation in the implementation c. of protection of women and children from acts of violence;

d. e. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence; providing Safe Houses for women and children victims of violence;

f. allocating budget for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence according to regional financial capacity; and

g. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;

h. establishing regional technical implementing units for protection of women and children based on the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 4

Every woman and child who becomes a victim of physical, psychological violence, exploitation or human trafficking is entitled to protection and services.

Protection, assistance, and services for victims as referred to in paragraph (1) include:

a. to be respected for their dignity as human beings;
b. complaints or requests for protection from the concerned party and/or other parties; c. confidential handling or placement of victims in safe locations and houses whose whereabouts are kept secret to avoid intimidation and threats;

medical services/health care in the form of d. treatment and recovery of injuries or physical condition of victims by medical and paramedical personnel; medicolegal services for evidentiary purposes e. in the legal field; protection by law enforcement officials; f. provision of information regarding legal rights and obligations ob. to victims or guardians of victims as well as identification of incidents; continuous handling up to the stage h.

Rehabilitation; recovery of physical, psychological

1. as well as sexual health according to the suffering experienced by victims of violence; psychological assistance to J. restore the traumatic condition of victims and restore victims' self-confidence;

k. assistance for legal processes and obtaining compensation or compensation rights; 1. provision of spiritual guidance services;

m. to make their own decisions;

n. assistance by social workers and legal aid at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;

i. guarantees of rights related to status as family members and community members (social reintegration); and L -D-F {,

j. economic independence services in the form of skills training and providing economic access so that victims can be independent.

(3) In the event that the victim is a child, in addition to obtaining the rights as referred to in paragraph (2), they also obtain special rights including:

a. the right to respect and full use for survival, growth and development;

the right to basic services; b. the right to equal protection;

C. the right to be free from various stigmas; d. the right to obtain freedom; and e. the right to obtain compensation from the perpetrator. f.

Article 19

(1) In implementing protection for women and children, the community may:

a. establish family partners at the kelurahan/village level by the community;
b. establish women and children protection units within community organizations;
c. conduct independent socialization of the rights of women and children; p.;ce.sq,&ffi :1 .:iljrr@,;'F "Ii-,i a-. 4.a\aI J _ 'ro: ia:E*!-:i:!1!:_r''rgt;rir'::r (- ' u'''L
i. .,,,i,,* '.;l\" i-* , , . ', i i,ti;t d. provide first aid to victims; and;
e. report to the competent authority if violence against victims occurs in their environment.
(2) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by volunteers, social community institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, the private sector, and mass media.

Article 16

(1) The Regional Government through the Dinas/UPTD is obligated to conduct economic empowerment for victims of acts of violence, exploitation and neglect.

(2) Forms of empowerment for victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), include:

a. procuring the necessary requirements for entrepreneurship training for victims of acts of violence, to improve business knowledge, attitude, and skills;
b. facilitating the implementation of various job training and skills training;
c. providing assistance in developing productive economic businesses;
d. exploring cooperation with small, medium and large enterprises, as well as financial institutions to develop businesses of victims of acts of violence;
e. endeavoring to provide capital for victims of acts of violence; and
f. expanding information access and promoting the products of victims of acts of violence.

CHAPTER XII

INVESTIGATION

Article 23

(1) In addition to general investigative officers, investigation of criminal acts of violence against women and children in this Regional Regulation may also be carried out by Civil Servant Investigators whose appointment is in accordance with the laws and regulations.

(2) Civil Servant Investigators as referred to in paragraph (1), are authorized to:

a. receive reports or complaints from a person regarding the occurrence of criminal acts of violence against women and children;
b. take first action at that time at the scene and conduct examination; c. interrogate a suspect and examine the suspect's identity;
d. seize objects and/or letters; e. take fingerprints and photograph a person; f. summon persons to be heard and examined as suspects or witnesses;
g. bring in experts needed in connection with the examination of the case;
h. terminate the investigation after receiving instructions from the General Investigator that there is insufficient evidence, or the incident does not constitute a criminal act and subsequently through the General Investigator notify this matter to the public prosecutor, the suspect or their family; and/or
i. take other actions necessary for the smooth investigation of criminal acts of violence against women and children, which according to law can be legally accounted for.
(3) Investigators as referred to in paragraph (2), notify the commencement of the investigation and submit the results of their investigation to the public prosecutor through the Investigating Officer of the Police of the Republic of Indonesia, in accordance with the provisions stipulated in the Criminal Procedure Law. v;

Article 15

Forms of services for the rights of women and children from acts of violence include:

a. complaint, consultation, and counseling services; b. assistance services;
c. health services;
d. social rehabilitation services;
e. legal services and/or legal aid; and f. repatriation and social reintegration services.

